  <!-- Other subsections for larger dividers -->
  <section xml:id="sec-assessments">
    <title>Assessments and Grades</title>

    <p>
<dl>
  

      <li>
        <title>Written Homework</title>
        <p>
          There will be 13 written homework assignments.  Directions for these, the grading rubric, and the specific exercises to address can be found under the <alert>Written Homework</alert> section of this website.  Remember that <em>doing</em> mathematics is the best, and perhaps <em>only</em> way to learn mathematics!  
        </p>

        <p>A syllabus quiz will be taken during the first week.  These points will count towards written homework.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
    <title>
      MOMwork
    </title>
  
  <p> Associated with the course is an open source (FREE) electronic homework system (MyOpenMath).  We will use this for MOMwork.  To access this, use the link <url href="https://www.myopenmath.com/" visual="https://www.myopenmath.com/"/>.
  </p> 

      <p>Course ID: <alert>323855</alert></p>
      <p>Enrollment Key: <alert>Buske312onlineF26</alert></p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Written Exams</title>
        <p>
          There will be seven (7) written exams.  One of the written exams will be a comprehensive final.  All exams are to be independent assessments of your knowledge.   
        </p>

        <p>
          <alert>Exam Proctoring:</alert> All exams in this course will be proctored (recorded via Respondus Monitor inside of D2L Brightspace), with no exceptions. 
        </p>

        <p> Exams (except the final exam) are time limited to 90 minutes, but may be taken during any 90-minute period of the scheduled day(s).  Students with possible schedule conflicts should contact me immediately to discuss other possible days at which a proctored exam could be taken. 
        </p>         

        <p> Exams will open as a D2L quiz and all written work will be scanned and submitted to a separate D2L assignment dropbox after the exam has closed in D2L.  More information is provided in D2L Brightspace.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li> Students will be required to install <term>Respondus Monitor</term> and <term>Respondus Lockdown Browser</term> on their computer before they can open the exams in D2L Brightspace.  This software then records audio and video (from two views) of each student during the exam, and these files are made available for viewing by the course instructor.  Hence, each student MUST have a camera connected to their computer that can be used to record video and audio during the exams.  
          </li>

          <li> An asynchronous D2L quiz has been assigned during the first week of class to verify that students have correctly installed the Respondus software on their computer and are able to view and complete a short D2L quiz.  All students must complete this asynchronous quiz by the given deadline or they will not be allowed access to any subsequent exam.
          </li>

          <li> During the exams, students will also be required to connect to the Respondus Lockdown Monitor via a second device, such as a cell phone or tablet.  <alert>This second device should be placed slightly behind and to one side of where the student is seated during the exam to clearly show that there are no unauthorized notes or devices being used during the exam.  Failure to do so will result in a score of zero on that exam.</alert>  Exam 0 is a trial run - passing Exam 0 means your setup is acceptable.  
          </li>
        </ul>


        <p> While some students may have reservations about the use of the Respondus software to record audio and video of themselves in their private living spaces, it is the unfortunate reality that academic conduct is so prevalent during unproctored online exams that it is absolutely necessary to protect the integrity of the testing process.  Our accreditors expect no less of us.   
        </p>

        <p> Students that prefer not to install the Respondus software may have the option to make arrangements to take the exams in person instead, either in an SCSU classroom with me if you live near the St. Cloud area or at another educational institution that provides test proctoring services.  Students must indicate their intent to do so during the first week of class, and all arrangements for proctoring at other educational institutions must be finalized no later than the end of the second week of class.
        </p>

        <p> Most, but not all, exams for this course will be closed-book and closed-notes, meaning that you may not use the textbook, any unauthorized notes or electronic devices, or any online resources while working on the exams nor may you discuss the exam problems with anyone.   All submitted work must be completed on your own without any unauthorized help.  Calculators will be allowed on exams; in fact, a scientific calculator will appear on the D2L screen during the exam.  
        </p>

        <p> Reminder: Submitting work from someone else, work generated by ChatGPT (or any other generative AI software), or work copied from other websites as your own would constitute a violation of the SCSU Academic Integrity policy, as would allowing another student to submit your work as their own.  Any such violation will be reported to the appropriate authority and will result in sanctions to the grades of all students involved as discussed in the SCSU Academic Integrity policy.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <title>Grading</title>
        <p>
          I will use my best professional judgment in determining grades. While many courses utilize 90%, 80%, 70%, and 60% as cutoffs for A, B, C, and D grades, these will serve only as guidelines.  Averages above these marks will guarantee these grades but lower marks may earn higher final grades.  
        </p>

        <table xml:id="GradeWeights" >
